WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:05.470
Joker, the upcoming standalone from Warner
Brothers and DC starring Joaquin Phoenix,

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:08.220
promises to be a comic book film unlike any
other.

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.620
Inspired heavily by the works of Martin Scorsese
and co-starring his frequent collaborator

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:17.420
Robert DeNiro, Joker presents the story of
Arthur Fleck, a failed comedian with a troubled

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:21.850
inner life who becomes the Clown Prince of
Crime, the most feared figure in Gotham City's

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:22.340
underworld.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:27.580
More character study than superpowered smash-up,
Joker is one of the rare comic book films

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:28.680
to receive an R rating.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.400
With a property as iconic as its main character,
getting clearance to push the film beyond

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.900
the boundaries of your average superhero fare
was not an easy task for co-writer and director

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.760
Todd Phillips.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:43.460
Talking with the Los Angeles Times, Phillips
explained why he decided to use the character

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:47.300
to tell a different kind of comic book story
on the big screen, and what it took to get

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.880
the green light to pursue his gritty, R-rated
vision.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.950
Phillips explained that the inspiration for
the film came as he was attending the premiere

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.140
of his 2016 dramedy War Dogs in August of
that year.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:01.320
Taking it all in, he couldn't help but be
distracted by a billboard for a big-budget

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.300
superhero movie which loomed over the area.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.900
He didn't say which one, but as the Times
noted, this was right around the time Suicide

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:11.920
Squad opened, which happened to feature a
supremely divisive turn by Jared Leto as the

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:12.500
Joker.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.120
Phillips may be known for comedies such as
The Hangover, but he's also a Scorsese disciple,

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.740
and said he had been disheartened by the last
couple decades' lack of in-depth character

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.510
studies for which the master filmmaker was
known such as 1974's Taxi Driver and 1980's

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:29.160
Raging Bull, both of which starred DeNiro.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:32.200
As he regarded the billboard, the seed of
an idea was formed.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:33.160
Phillips said,

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.900
"I knew that War Dogs wasn't going to set
the world on fire, and I was thinking, 'What

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.520
do people really want to see?'

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:43.040
The movies that I grew up loving, these character
studies from the '70s, you couldn't get those

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.520
movies made in this climate.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:48.420
I'm staring up at this billboard and I said
to myself, 'What if you did a movie in that

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:50.720
vein, but made it about one of those characters?'"

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.200
"I'm an ideeaaaaa."

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.640
Perhaps inspired by the character's Suicide
Squad depiction, Phillips became focused on

00:01:59.649 --> 00:02:03.970
the Joker, and as he discussed his idea with
the film's co-writer Scott Silver, the pair

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.409
were emboldened by the fact that the iconic
Batman nemesis had never received a proper

00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:11.140
origin story in the comics, which was by design.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.400
While various depictions of the character
including those in Tim Burton's Batman and

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.760
the TV series Gotham had settled on the "falling
into a vat of chemicals while escaping the

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.719
Dark Knight" tack, Phillips and Silver decided
that it would be much more interesting to

00:02:23.719 --> 00:02:28.109
offer up their own version of the Joker's
origin, one far less fanciful and much more

00:02:28.109 --> 00:02:29.280
grounded in reality.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:30.740
Phillips said,

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.340
"We wanted to look at everything through as
real and authentic a lens as possible.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.019
I don't believe that in the real world if
you fell into a vat of acid you would turn

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.359
white and have a smile and your hair would
be green."

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.080
"You idiot!"

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:48.160
Instead, Phillips went about reverse-engineering
the story, giving the Joker an origin story

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:49.760
that was slightly more realistic.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.639
It was an enjoyable process.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.820
According to the filmmaker,

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:58.180
"It was one of the most fun scripts to write,
because you were only breaking rules."

00:02:58.199 --> 00:03:01.980
In 2009, Phillips made The Hangover for Warner
Brothers, and since that comparatively low-budget

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.999
comedy went on to gross an improbable half-billion
dollars worldwide, the studio's executives

00:03:06.999 --> 00:03:11.040
were inclined to hear him out on his vision
for Joker, certain story elements of which

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.000
would require the film to carry an R rating.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.159
But there were seemingly a million different
objections to the idea that had to be dealt

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:19.720
with before Phillips and his team were allowed
to proceed.

00:03:20.180 --> 00:03:24.250
Complicating matters was the fact that a major
regime change had taken place at Warner Brothers

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:26.200
in the middle of the movie's pre-production.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:27.640
Phillips recalled,

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.060
"It was a yearlong process from when we finished
the script just to get the new people on board

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.860
with this vision, because I pitched it to
an entirely different team than made it.

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:39.439
There were emails about: 'You realize we sell
Joker pajamas at Target.'

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:42.960
There were a zillion hurdles, and you just
sort of had to navigate those one at a time."

00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:47.639
The process was difficult and frustrating,
but Phillips said it was worth it, and that

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.640
he's still kind of surprised that he got away
with it all.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:50.500
He said,

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.220
"At the time, I would curse them in my head
every day.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.160
But then I have to put it in perspective and
go, 'They're pretty bold that they did this.'"

00:03:57.460 --> 00:04:02.279
Bold, yes, but it's shaping up to be a pretty
good call: Joker is the most highly-anticipated

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.380
film of the fall, and Phoenix's performance
is generating strong Oscar buzz.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.440
It's a radically different approach to the
DC Comics properties than producers had been

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:14.670
pursuing with their shared universe, and may
represent a new way forward for the sometimes

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:15.670
embattled brand.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:17.769
This fact isn't lost on Phillips.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:21.780
Midway through Joker's production, he suggested
to Warner Brothers executives that they create

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:26.120
an entirely new label for gritty character
studies such as his take on the Joker: a new

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:27.840
banner called DC Black.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:30.740
According to Phillips, they weren't immediately
on board.

00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:31.980
He said the response was,

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.880
"'Calm down with the label — how about you
do one movie?'"

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.580
Despite the positive reactions, Phillips understands
that his interpretation of the Joker might

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.949
not be every DC fan's cup of tea.

00:04:41.949 --> 00:04:45.349
That's just fine with him, because he knows
that his take on the Clown Prince of Crime

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:49.000
will in no way be the definitive one, and
it certainly won't be the final one.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:49.740
He said,

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:52.360
"This will not be the last Joker movie ever
made.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.960
It might be the last one Joaquin and I do,
but someone else is going to come along and

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.720
do another one, just like with Spider-Man.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.160
So if you don't like this one, don't worry
it'll get reinvented again.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:05.259
[...] They've done four or five versions of
the Joker in the last 25 or 30 years.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:07.640
So why not do another one that's wildly different?"

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:09.020
Why not, indeed?

